/*!

@page first_window Open a window

@tableofcontents

This is the shortest complete Lumina program: about fifty lines that open a
window, fill it with a colour and exit when you close it or press _Escape_.  It
is the page to read first.  Nothing else in this documentation is a prerequisite
for it.

The code is not an excerpt written for the page.  It is `examples/minimal.c`,
which is compiled every time Lumina's examples are built, so it cannot drift out
of date.

@section first_window_whole The whole program

@snippet minimal.c code

That is the entire program.  If you already have Lumina building, copy it into a
file, build it as described in @ref first_window_build below, and you have a
window.

The rest of this page explains each part of it.


@section first_window_walk What each part does

@subsection first_window_include Including the headers

@code
#define GLAD_GL_IMPLEMENTATION
#include <glad/gl.h>
#define LUM_INCLUDE_NONE
#include <Lumina/lumina.h>
@endcode

The only header Lumina itself needs is `Lumina/lumina.h`.  The two other lines
bring in [glad](https://gen.glad.sh/), an extension loader library, because the
OpenGL header shipped with your development environment may be very old — on
Windows it typically stops at OpenGL 1.2.

`LUM_INCLUDE_NONE` tells Lumina not to include an OpenGL header of its own,
since glad has already provided one.  You can leave both glad lines out if you
are happy with whatever OpenGL version your system header exposes, or if you are
not drawing anything yet.  See @ref build_include for the full set of options.

@subsection first_window_errors Hearing about errors

@code
static void error_callback(int error, const char* description)
{
    fprintf(stderr, "Lumina error: %s\n", description);
}
@endcode

Set with @ref lumSetErrorCallback, this is called whenever a Lumina function
fails, with a human-readable description of what went wrong.  It is the first
thing to set, because it works even before the library is initialized, and
without it a failure tells you only that something returned `NULL`.

@subsection first_window_init Initializing the library

@code
if (!lumInit())
    return EXIT_FAILURE;
@endcode

@ref lumInit must succeed before any other function except the error callback
and a few version queries.  @ref lumTerminate, at the other end of the program,
undoes it and releases everything Lumina allocated.

@subsection first_window_create Creating the window

@code
window = lumCreateWindowEx(&(LUMwindowdesc)
{
    .width       = 640,
    .height      = 480,
    .title       = "My first Lumina window",
    .makeCurrent = LUM_ON
});
@endcode

@ref lumCreateWindowEx creates a window together with an OpenGL context, and
takes everything it needs from a @ref LUMwindowdesc.  __Every field left out
takes the library default__, so only the four that matter here are written: the
size of the content area, the window title, and `makeCurrent`, which asks for
the new context to be made current on this thread right away.

Three fields are required — `width`, `height` and `title`.  Everything else,
from the OpenGL version to the number of samples, has a sensible default.  The
@ref window_creation_desc section of the window guide covers the whole
descriptor; @ref LUMwindowdesc lists every field.

@code
if (!window)
{
    lumTerminate();
    return EXIT_FAILURE;
}
@endcode

Window creation can fail — most often because the requested OpenGL version is
not available — and when it does it returns `NULL` after calling your error
callback.

@subsection first_window_key Reacting to the keyboard

@code
static void key_callback(LUMwindow* window,
                         int key, int scancode, int action, int mods)
{
    if (key == LUM_KEY_ESCAPE && action == LUM_PRESS)
        lumSetWindowShouldClose(window, LUM_TRUE);
}
@endcode

Input in Lumina arrives through callbacks you write and register, here with
@ref lumSetKeyCallback.  This one watches for _Escape_ and sets the window's
close flag, which is exactly what clicking the close button does.  The
@ref input_guide covers the rest: text, mouse, scrolling, gamepads and more.

@subsection first_window_loader Loading the OpenGL functions

@code
gladLoadGL(lumGetProcAddress);
lumSwapInterval(1);
@endcode

OpenGL functions beyond the oldest ones have to be looked up at run time.
@ref lumGetProcAddress does the looking up and glad does the storing, so one
call wires the two together.  This has to happen after a context is current,
which `makeCurrent` already arranged.

Passing `1` to @ref lumSwapInterval asks for buffer swaps to wait for the next
vertical blank, which caps the loop at the monitor's refresh rate instead of
letting it spin as fast as it can.

@subsection first_window_loop The event loop

@code
while (!lumWindowShouldClose(window))
{
    int width, height;
    lumGetFramebufferSize(window, &width, &height);
    glViewport(0, 0, width, height);

    glClearColor(0.18f, 0.12f, 0.30f, 1.f);
    glClear(GL_COLOR_BUFFER_BIT);

    lumSwapBuffers(window);
    lumPollEvents();
}
@endcode

@ref lumWindowShouldClose reports the close flag, so the loop ends when the user
closes the window or presses _Escape_.

Inside it, @ref lumGetFramebufferSize gives the size of the drawing area in
pixels.  Ask for it every frame rather than remembering it: the window can be
resized, and on a high-DPI display it is not the same as the size you asked for
in screen coordinates.

@ref lumSwapBuffers presents the frame you just drew, and @ref lumPollEvents
processes everything that has arrived from the operating system since the last
call, which is what makes your callbacks run.  Without it the window would never
respond to anything.

@subsection first_window_clean Shutting down

@code
lumDestroyWindow(window);
lumTerminate();
@endcode

@ref lumTerminate destroys any remaining windows by itself, so the explicit call
to @ref lumDestroyWindow is not strictly required here — but being explicit
costs one line and keeps the shape of the program clear.


@section first_window_build Building and running it

Lumina has to be built before anything can link against it, and the two things
are separate steps:

- @ref compile_guide builds the library itself.  With CMake it is three
  commands.
- @ref build_guide links your program against it, from CMake, pkg-config, or by
  hand.

If you are building Lumina from this repository, the example above is already
there.  Configure with examples enabled and the binary is built for you:

@code{.sh}
cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build --target minimal
@endcode

The program then lives under `build/examples`.  Run it and you get a small
violet window that closes on _Escape_.

@section first_window_next Where to go next

- @ref quick_guide is the longer version of this page.  It builds up to a
  rotating triangle and introduces more of the API along the way.
- @ref intro_guide explains the rules the whole API follows: initialization,
  error handling, versioning and thread safety.
- @ref window_guide covers windows in full — the complete descriptor, full
  screen, icons, resizing, attributes and all the window callbacks.
- @ref input_guide covers everything the user can do: keyboard, text, mouse,
  cursors, files dropped onto the window, joysticks and gamepads.
- @ref vulkan_guide shows what changes when you render with Vulkan instead of
  OpenGL.  It is mostly one field: `.context = LUM_OFF`.

*/
